    <article>
      <section id="problem" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
        <h2>The app opens only when money has to move</h2>
        <p>People aged 21 to 30 in India split dinners, trips and flat bills with the same three to twelve friends. For them, Google Pay opens only when money has to move. A nicer split screen improves that moment but never creates a second one, and retention needs a second moment.</p>
        <p>Groups already exist, three taps deep behind a chat menu, and an active group never shows up on Home. The split is a total and a rule: evenly, by amounts, by shares or by percentages. No items, no who had what.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="found" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">What I found</p>
        <div class="stats">
          <div><b>50 million</b><span>installs Tez drove with scratch cards, which faded once the rewards thinned</span></div>
          <div><b>3 taps</b><span>how deep groups sit in Google Pay today, behind a chat menu</span></div>
          <div><b>1 trigger</b><span>the only reason to come back is Send reminder, which the app itself calls a private reminder</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>Venmo showed the other side: people open it multiple times a week just to see what friends are doing, and the note on each split is the content. A Google Pay review from July 2026 named the gap from the user’s side: after a few days, older split payments become hard to find. The record exists and gets buried.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="hardest">
        <p class="eyebrow">The hardest call</p>
        <h2>Kill the reminder</h2>
        <p>It was the one return trigger the app had, and I removed it. A reminder is a nag, and it feels bad for both people. You cannot bolt fun onto nagging. So the request becomes a board both people see, and social visibility does the reminder’s job.</p>
        <blockquote>A split does not end at send. Give it a <em>lifecycle</em>.</blockquote>
      </section>

      <section id="system" class="lt wide">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:22px;max-width:66ch">
          <p class="eyebrow">The system</p>
          <h2>Five flows around one idea: a split that stays alive</h2>
          <p>Flow 1 is a small group, item by item. Flow 2 is a big group where twelve people claim in parallel. Flow 3 is a bill with no receipt. Flow 4 is the person who owes. Flow 5 is the whole notification loop. Every path ends in the same place: a reason to come back.</p>
        </div>
        <figure>
          <a class="zoom" href="img/00-user-flow.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the user flow full size"><img class="shot wide" src="img/00-user-flow.webp" width="5000" height="3722" alt="Split and Request user flow: five flows around one split" loading="lazy"><span class="zi" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M15 3h6v6M9 21H3v-6M21 3l-7 7M3 21l7-7"/></svg></span></a><button type="button" class="flowfs" aria-label="Open the user flow full screen"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M15 3h6v6M9 21H3v-6M21 3l-7 7M3 21l7-7"/></svg>Full screen</button>
          <dialog class="flowdlg" aria-label="User flow, full screen"><button type="button" class="flowx" aria-label="Close full screen"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M18 6L6 18M6 6l12 12"/></svg></button><div class="flowpan"><img src="img/00-user-flow.webp" width="5000" height="3722" alt="Split and Request user flow: five flows around one split" loading="lazy"></div><p class="flowhint">Pinch or double tap to zoom</p></dialog>
          <figcaption>Green paths are the happy route, red paths are the fallback. Every box is a screen in the Figma file.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p><a class="tl" href="https://www.figma.com/board/5lCaixaqTJpvqKOogiZjYD/GPay-Split-%E2%80%94-User-Flow--all-five-journeys-" target="_blank" rel="noopener">See the flow in Figma</a></p>
      </section>

      <section id="flow" class="lt wide">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:22px;max-width:66ch">
          <p class="eyebrow">Flow 1, end to end</p>
          <h2>Making the split, then everything after it</h2>
          <p>The first four screens make the split fun to do. The next four are the lifecycle: the reasons to come back after money has moved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="screens">
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="shot phone" src="img/01-bill-in.webp" width="780" height="1720" alt="Bill in: scan or type" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="k">01 · Bill in</span><h3>Scan or type</h3><p>Scan the receipt and every dish comes in on its own, or type the total when there is no bill.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="shot phone" src="img/02-plates-nothing-claimed.webp" width="780" height="1972" alt="Who had what: everyone starts at zero" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="k">02 · Who had what</span><h3>Everyone starts at zero</h3><p>My first version was a checkbox list and it read like a hospital form. The reveal is the fun part: whose plate is heavy, who claimed nothing.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="shot phone" src="img/03-drag-a-dish.webp" width="780" height="1972" alt="Drag a dish onto a face" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="k">03 · Drag a dish onto a face</span><h3>Totals move as you go</h3><p>Drag each dish onto a person and the totals move as you assign. Claimed items stay tappable, so two people on the naan divide it automatically. Nobody gets greyed out of a dish they shared.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure class="pop"><img src="img/03-drag-a-dish.webp" width="780" height="1972" alt="" loading="lazy"><img src="img/03a-shared-dish-sheet.webp" width="780" height="1004" alt="Shared dish sheet open over the screen" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="k">Pop up · Shared dish</span><h3>Garlic naan × 6, four people</h3><p>Tap any dish still on the table and this sheet opens. Everyone who ate some gets their pieces, and the money follows the pieces, ₹120 each here. Done stays locked until the sheet says “All 6 pieces accounted for”.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="shot phone" src="img/04-everything-claimed.webp" width="780" height="1980" alt="Everything claimed" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="k">04 · Everything claimed</span><h3>Nothing left on the table</h3><p>One tap to send. Split evenly stays one tap away on every screen, because fun you cannot opt out of is a tax.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure class="pop"><img src="img/04-everything-claimed.webp" width="780" height="1980" alt="" loading="lazy"><img src="img/04a-send-request-sheet.webp" width="780" height="896" alt="Send request sheet open over the screen" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="k">Pop up · Send request</span><h3>One last look before it goes</h3><p>Tap Send request and everyone’s line shows up with the dishes behind it, so nobody gets a number they have to question. Your own ₹890 stays with you, so the request is ₹2,910, not ₹3,800.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="shot phone" src="img/05-open-board.webp" width="780" height="1678" alt="Open: the request becomes a board" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="state open">Open</span><h3>The request becomes a board</h3><p>It does not end at send. Everyone sees the same board: who paid, who has seen it, who has not.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="shot phone" src="img/06-paid-moment.webp" width="780" height="1756" alt="Paid: good news on the board" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="state paid">Paid</span><h3>Good news, not a receipt</h3><p>When someone pays, it arrives as good news on the board everyone shares.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="shot phone" src="img/07-settled-memory.webp" width="780" height="1912" alt="Settled: the split becomes a memory" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="state settled">Settled</span><h3>Once it settles, it becomes a memory</h3><p>The total, how long it took, who paid first, and photos from the group. Photos earn their place here because the memory needs somewhere to live.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="shot phone" src="img/08-monthly-recap.webp" width="780" height="1964" alt="A month later: the monthly recap" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="state recap">A month later</span><h3>It comes back as a recap</h3><p>A month later the group gets a recap of where they went and who picked up the most. Four states, four reasons to open the app, and none of them need money to move.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="scale" class="wide">
        <div class="screens">
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="shot phone" src="img/09-claiming-live.webp" width="780" height="1884" alt="Big group: everyone claims live" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><p class="eyebrow">Flow 2 · Big group</p><h2>Twelve people. Fifty items.</h2><p>Twelve people and fifty items break any assigning screen. Bulk assign was my first attempt and it still took fifty decisions. So the organiser stops assigning, and everyone claims their own, live, on their own phone. Claiming online also means it works after everyone has gone home, or for a trip or a grocery run.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure class="pop"><img src="img/09-claiming-live.webp" width="780" height="1884" alt="" loading="lazy"><img src="img/09a-assign-leftovers.webp" width="780" height="888" alt="Assign the leftovers open over the screen" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><span class="k">Pop up · Leftovers</span><h3>Two items nobody claimed</h3><p>While the group claims, the organiser can pick what is still unclaimed and tap “Give 2 selected to someone”. Choose who had the sangria and nachos and the sheet splits them equally, ₹655 each.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="owes" class="lt wide">
        <div class="screens">
          <div class="screen owes">
            <figure class="right"><div class="locks">
                <div><span class="k">Lock screen · Small group</span><h3>“Your bit is ₹350”</h3><p>Kabir sees his exact share and what it was for, the cold coffee and gulab jamun. One tap opens the same board everyone else sees.</p><img src="img/10b-notification-small-group.webp" width="780" height="276" alt="Small group lock screen notification" loading="lazy"></div>
                <div><span class="k">Lock screen · Big group</span><h3>“Tap what you had at Office Lunch”</h3><p>Fifty items and twelve people, so nobody gets a number yet. Kabir is asked to claim his own, and the message promises it takes a minute.</p><img src="img/10-notification.webp" width="780" height="276" alt="Big group lock screen notification" loading="lazy"></div>
                <div><span class="k">Lock screen · Nudge</span><h3>“Kabir hasn’t opened the split”</h3><p>Forty minutes later he gets a nudge, not a nag. If nobody taps their items, the leftovers split evenly, so the bill never waits on one person.</p><img src="img/10c-notification-nudge.webp" width="780" height="276" alt="Nudge lock screen notification" loading="lazy"></div>
              </div>
              <img class="shot phone" src="img/10a-their-board.webp" width="780" height="2124" alt="Their view of the same board" loading="lazy">
            </figure>
            
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow">Flow 4 · The person who owes</p>
              <h2>One notification, the same board</h2>
              <p>The person who owes gets one notification and opens the same board, not a payment sheet that closes. They review their own items and pay from inside it, and the board is still there afterwards.</p>
              
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="receipt" class="lt wide">
        <div class="screens">
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="shot phone" src="img/11-no-receipt.webp" width="780" height="1678" alt="No receipt: type the total and split evenly" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div><p class="eyebrow">Flow 3 · No receipt</p><h2>Nothing to itemise</h2><p>No bill, nothing to itemise. Type the total and split evenly, and the item by item option is not shown at all.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="groups" class="lt wide">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:22px;max-width:66ch">
          <p class="eyebrow">Surface what exists</p>
          <h2>The group was already there</h2>
          <p>Groups already existed three taps deep. They move above People on Home, and the group page leads with the balance and memories, with settings at the bottom. Nothing needed inventing, it needed surfacing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pair">
          <a class="zoom crop" href="img/12-home.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the full Home screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/12-home.webp" width="780" height="4520" alt="Home with Groups moved above People" loading="lazy"><span class="zi" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M15 3h6v6M9 21H3v-6M21 3l-7 7M3 21l7-7"/></svg></span></a>
          <img class="shot phone" src="img/12a-group-page.webp" width="780" height="2824" alt="Group page: balance and memories first" loading="lazy">
        </div>
      </section>

