      <section id="decisions" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">Decisions</p>
        <h2>Every call, and what I turned down to make it</h2>
        <div class="trades">
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">01</span><div><h3>Solve the split, not the whole app</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>The one action people already do is the only surface worth investing in</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>Surfacing rewards, offers and bill categories</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">02</span><div><h3>Give the request a lifecycle</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>Four states means four reasons to come back without money moving</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>Making the split screen prettier and calling it the fix</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">03</span><div><h3>Kill the reminder</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>A shared board replaces a private nag</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>Softening the reminder copy or adding emoji to it</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">04</span><div><h3>Items onto faces, not a form</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>The reveal of who had what is the joke people already make at the table</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>An itemised list with dropdowns</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">05</span><div><h3>Tap to join, never grey out</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>Sharing a dish is normal, so no one gets locked out</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>Disabling items once claimed</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">06</span><div><h3>At scale, everyone claims their own</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>Parallel claiming turns data entry into the social part</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>Bulk assign, which still took fifty decisions</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">07</span><div><h3>Online claiming, not pass the phone</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>It works after people leave the table, and for trips and grocery runs</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>Handing the phone around</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">08</span><div><h3>One board, same for everyone</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>A payment sheet that closes leaves nothing behind</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>A separate screen for the person who owes</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">09</span><div><h3>Surface the group that already exists</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>Groups were three taps deep, they needed surfacing, not inventing</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>A new social layer built from scratch</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">10</span><div><h3>Photos, because the memory needs somewhere to live</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>A photo earns its place only where the memory lives</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>Photos as decoration with nowhere to go</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">11</span><div><h3>Every fun idea passes one test</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>Would anyone open the app less next month without it?</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>Reward fun tied to money rather than people</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">12</span><div><h3>Itemising is always optional</h3><p><span class="k">Why</span>Fun you cannot opt out of is a tax</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">Rejected</span>Item by item as the only path</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="funtest"><span class="k">The test every fun idea had to pass</span><p>If you removed it, would anyone open the app less next month? Confetti, stickers, scratch cards and reactions on requests all failed and were cut. Tez showed why: scratch cards drove 50 million installs and faded once the rewards thinned. Reward fun runs out. People fun does not.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section id="assumptions" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">Assumptions</p>
        <p>Amounts, names and photos are illustrative. Receipt scanning assumes the bill comes in as line items. Everyone in the split is assumed to be on the same app.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="changed" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">What changed</p>
        <h2>The same split, four more moments</h2>
        <div class="beforeafter">
          <div class="b"><span class="k">Before</span><p>A split ends the moment it is sent, and the only way back is a reminder.</p></div>
          <div class="a"><span class="k">After</span><p>The same split returns four times, as a board, good news, a memory and a recap.</p></div>
        </div>
        <span class="k meask">How I would measure it, in the 21 to 30 cohort</span>
        <div class="list">
          <div><span class="n">1</span><h3>Weekly opens that no money request triggered</h3></div>
          <div><span class="n">2</span><h3>Share of splits made item by item</h3></div>
          <div><span class="n">3</span><h3>Median time for a split to settle</h3></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="learned">
        <p class="eyebrow">What I learned</p>
        <h2>The fix was a longer life for an old feature</h2>
        <p>The biggest lesson: the fix for retention was not a new feature but a longer life for one people already use.</p>
        <div class="still"><span class="k">Still open</span>
        <p>Nothing stops someone from removing all their own claims, so a real build needs a confirm step. If two people claim the same item and disagree, I have not designed that moment yet. The big group flow has no monthly recap yet. That is an omission, not a decision.</p></div>
      </section>
    </article>
